  "query_preview": "..Slightly above normal hghts will persist through \nThursday, delivering seasonably warm daytime temperatures and \nsouthwesterly breezes each afternoon.\n\nBy early Friday, the flow aloft turns more southwesterly and once \nagain, tropical moisture will get pulled into the area heading into \nthe weekend. This pattern is not quite as clear-cut as the last few \nevents, but has the potential to really soak our state. \n\nThe first wave of moisture on Friday is almost purely tropical. \nExpect a generalized light rainfall for most places and without much \nin the way of dynamics, thunderstorm activity will be limited. On \nSaturday, the moisture stream continues, but almost nothing in terms \nof large scale ascent. So, mainly a drippy day, with showers tied to \nterrain.\n\nBy Sunday, a much larger slug of tropical moisture arrives, all \nwhile the northern stream trough finally makes its move into the \ninterior west. Many things have to come together to get this right \non Sunday and phasing issues have already been noted. However, the \npotential with this final wave is for widespread heavier showers and \nthunderstorms. Stronger lift and shear comes into play, which may \nproduce isolated severe thunderstorms.\n\nA few showers may linger into Monday as the trough moves east, but \nin general, the remainder of next week looks like a new ridge will \ndominate, with a return to fair and seasonably warm conditions.",
